1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,130
April 30th, 1943.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:05,430
Four fifteen in the morning.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:10,170
Just off the coast of southern Spain,
a British submarine rises quietly to

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,110
the surface of a black sea.

5
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:19,489
That spring, British intelligence had sent a
man on a secret mission to deceive Nazi Germany.

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:23,529
He carried confidential
documents, a military identity,

7
00:00:23,559 --> 00:00:26,666
and a story that could
change the course of the war.

8
00:00:27,616 --> 00:00:30,086
The Germans were supposed to find him.

9
00:00:30,116 --> 00:00:32,011
They were supposed to believe him.

10
00:00:33,261 --> 00:00:35,205
There was just one problem.

11
00:00:37,555 --> 00:00:39,228
He was already dead.

12
00:00:41,378 --> 00:00:44,648
This is the story of the
dead man who fooled Hitler,

13
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:47,968
and of the real man,
forgotten for half a century,

14
00:00:47,998 --> 00:00:49,931
whose body made it possible.

15
00:00:55,281 --> 00:00:59,671
At around nine thirty that same morning,
a Spanish fisherman working the water

16
00:00:59,701 --> 00:01:03,051
near Huelva saw something
floating in the swell.

17
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:06,090
It was the body of a
man in British uniform.

18
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:11,790
Spanish soldiers carried him to Huelva
and handed him to a naval judge.

19
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:15,230
The papers in his pockets
identified him as Captain,

20
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:19,070
Acting Major, William
Martin of the Royal Marines.

21
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:23,333
Attached to him by a leather-covered
chain was an official briefcase.

22
00:01:24,183 --> 00:01:27,673
Everything about him
suggested a tragedy at sea.

23
00:01:27,703 --> 00:01:32,293
An officer, perhaps a passenger
on an aircraft lost off the coast,

24
00:01:32,323 --> 00:01:35,388
washed ashore with the
papers he had been carrying.

25
00:01:36,238 --> 00:01:40,988
The British vice-consul in Huelva,
Francis Haselden, was informed.

26
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:46,448
Coded messages began to pass between him
and the British naval attaché in Madrid.

27
00:01:46,478 --> 00:01:48,108
Their tone was urgent.

28
00:01:48,138 --> 00:01:50,644
The briefcase, they implied, mattered.

29
00:01:51,494 --> 00:01:55,984
Within days, the papers inside it would
pass through Spanish hands and into

30
00:01:56,014 --> 00:01:58,544
the files of German intelligence.

31
00:01:58,574 --> 00:02:03,113
Within two weeks, Adolf Hitler himself
would be weighing what they said.

32
00:02:03,963 --> 00:02:06,233
So who was Major William Martin?

33
00:02:06,263 --> 00:02:08,453
Why was he carrying secrets?

34
00:02:08,483 --> 00:02:11,988
And why did British intelligence
want the enemy to read them?

35
00:02:13,038 --> 00:02:15,628
To answer that, we have
to go back to London,

36
00:02:15,658 --> 00:02:21,378
to a body in a mortuary, and to one of the
strangest plans of the Second World War.

37
00:02:26,328 --> 00:02:31,978
By the end of 1942, the war in the
Mediterranean had begun to turn.

38
00:02:32,008 --> 00:02:35,718
Allied armies were driving
the Axis out of North Africa,

39
00:02:35,748 --> 00:02:40,318
and planners in London and Washington
were already asking the next question:

40
00:02:40,348 --> 00:02:41,929
where to strike at Europe.

41
00:02:42,779 --> 00:02:49,049
A cross-Channel invasion of France
was judged impossible before 1944.

42
00:02:49,079 --> 00:02:53,409
Winston Churchill wanted to use the
armies in North Africa to attack what

43
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:55,797
he called Europe's soft underbelly.

44
00:02:56,647 --> 00:02:58,917
There were two obvious choices.

45
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:03,997
The first was Sicily, at the toe of Italy,
less than a hundred miles from Tunisia.

46
00:03:04,027 --> 00:03:06,897
Taking it would open the
Mediterranean to Allied shipping,

47
00:03:06,927 --> 00:03:10,357
and offer a stepping
stone into Italy itself.

48
00:03:10,387 --> 00:03:14,377
The second was Greece and the Balkans,
where an invasion could threaten the

49
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:19,369
oil, copper, chrome and bauxite
that fed Germany's war industry.

50
00:03:20,219 --> 00:03:23,269
In January 1943, at the
Casablanca Conference,

51
00:03:23,299 --> 00:03:26,569
the Allies made their choice.

52
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:28,589
The target would be Sicily.

53
00:03:28,619 --> 00:03:34,423
The invasion was code-named Operation Husky,
and it would be launched no later than July.

54
00:03:35,273 --> 00:03:37,843
But Sicily was the obvious choice.

55
00:03:37,873 --> 00:03:40,283
Anyone looking at a map could see it.

56
00:03:40,313 --> 00:03:45,883
Churchill is reputed to have said that everyone
but a bloody fool would know it was Sicily.

57
00:03:45,913 --> 00:03:48,823
If German divisions were
waiting on its beaches,

58
00:03:48,853 --> 00:03:51,448
the cost of the landings
could be enormous.

59
00:03:52,298 --> 00:03:54,608
The Allies needed Hitler
to look somewhere else,

60
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:56,948
and they knew where he
was already looking.

61
00:03:56,978 --> 00:03:59,548
Hitler feared an attack in the Balkans.

62
00:03:59,578 --> 00:04:03,248
So the Allies built a wider
deception, code-named Barclay,

63
00:04:03,278 --> 00:04:07,428
to feed that fear: a fictional
Twelfth Army based in Cairo,

64
00:04:07,458 --> 00:04:11,048
dummy tanks in Syria, Greek
interpreters recruited,

65
00:04:11,078 --> 00:04:13,768
Greek maps and currency stockpiled.

66
00:04:14,618 --> 00:04:19,368
But dummy armies and false radio
traffic could only do so much.

67
00:04:19,398 --> 00:04:22,568
What the planners needed was
evidence the enemy would believe,

68
00:04:22,598 --> 00:04:25,823
because the enemy had
found it for themselves.

69
00:04:30,573 --> 00:04:33,303
The idea was not entirely new.

70
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:38,023
In September 1939, a memo circulated
by the Director of Naval Intelligence,

71
00:04:38,053 --> 00:04:44,283
Admiral John Godfrey, listed dozens
of ways to deceive the enemy.

72
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:48,503
Historians have noted that it bore the
hallmarks of his personal assistant,

73
00:04:48,533 --> 00:04:53,312
a young officer named Ian Fleming,
the future creator of James Bond.

74
00:04:54,162 --> 00:04:57,612
Item number twenty-eight
carried a warning in its title:

75
00:04:57,642 --> 00:05:00,632
a suggestion, not a very nice one.

76
00:05:00,662 --> 00:05:03,552
It proposed planting
misleading papers on a corpse,

77
00:05:03,582 --> 00:05:05,596
to be found by the enemy.

78
00:05:06,646 --> 00:05:13,236
In September 1942, an aircraft flying from
Britain to Gibraltar crashed off Cádiz.

79
00:05:13,266 --> 00:05:17,216
Among the dead was a British
courier carrying secret documents.

80
00:05:17,246 --> 00:05:20,256
His body washed up on the Spanish coast.

81
00:05:20,286 --> 00:05:23,556
When it was returned, his
letter appeared unopened.

82
00:05:23,586 --> 00:05:28,325
But other evidence showed that material
recovered in Spain was reaching the Germans.

83
00:05:29,175 --> 00:05:35,305
A month later, an RAF officer seconded
to MI5 put the idea on paper again.

84
00:05:35,335 --> 00:05:39,425
His name was Charles Cholmondeley,
secretary of the Twenty Committee,

85
00:05:39,455 --> 00:05:43,105
the small secret group that
ran Britain's double agents.

86
00:05:43,135 --> 00:05:45,506
He called his plan Trojan Horse.

87
00:05:46,356 --> 00:05:51,266
The committee turned it down as
unworkable, but saw potential in the idea.

88
00:05:51,296 --> 00:05:55,186
Because the plan had a naval connection,
its chairman paired Cholmondeley

89
00:05:55,216 --> 00:05:59,336
with the committee's naval
representative: Ewen Montagu.

90
00:06:00,186 --> 00:06:05,536
Montagu was a barrister and King's Counsel
who had volunteered when the war began.

91
00:06:05,566 --> 00:06:10,596
In Naval Intelligence, he ran the
section that handled counter-espionage.

92
00:06:10,626 --> 00:06:14,696
Together, he and Cholmondeley set out
to discover what a plan like this would

93
00:06:14,726 --> 00:06:16,199
actually require.

94
00:06:17,049 --> 00:06:20,299
First, they consulted Britain's
most famous pathologist,

95
00:06:20,329 --> 00:06:22,199
Sir Bernard Spilsbury.

96
00:06:22,229 --> 00:06:26,139
Could a body convince a Spanish
doctor that a man had died at sea?

97
00:06:26,169 --> 00:06:28,659
Spilsbury's answer encouraged them.

98
00:06:28,689 --> 00:06:33,119
Victims of air crashes over water often
died of shock rather than drowning.

99
00:06:33,149 --> 00:06:37,959
And in Spain, he advised, detailed
post-mortems were rarely carried out unless

100
00:06:37,989 --> 00:06:40,275
the cause of death seemed important.

101
00:06:41,125 --> 00:06:45,335
Then they needed the body itself: someone
whose death would raise no questions,

102
00:06:45,365 --> 00:06:48,415
and whom no one would
come forward to claim.

103
00:06:48,445 --> 00:06:52,375
Montagu turned to Bentley
Purchase, a London coroner.

104
00:06:52,405 --> 00:06:55,575
Bodies were not in short
supply in wartime London,

105
00:06:55,605 --> 00:06:56,895
Purchase told him.

106
00:06:56,925 --> 00:06:59,574
But every single one
had to be accounted for.

107
00:07:00,424 --> 00:07:05,574
On the 28th of January
1943, Purchase got in touch.

108
00:07:05,604 --> 00:07:07,854
He had found a body that might work.

109
00:07:09,004 --> 00:07:13,514
A week later, Montagu and Cholmondeley
submitted their plan to the Twenty Committee.

110
00:07:13,544 --> 00:07:15,074
It needed a name.

111
00:07:15,104 --> 00:07:18,394
Montagu took one from a list
of available code words:

112
00:07:18,424 --> 00:07:20,086
Operation Mincemeat.

113
00:07:25,036 --> 00:07:27,186
The man in the mortuary had a name.

114
00:07:27,216 --> 00:07:30,574
For more than fifty years,
almost no one knew it.

115
00:07:31,724 --> 00:07:37,254
He was Glyndwr Michael, from Aberbargoed, a
mining town in the valleys of South Wales.

116
00:07:37,284 --> 00:07:41,734
His father, a coal miner, took his
own life when Glyndwr was fifteen.

117
00:07:41,764 --> 00:07:44,094
His mother died when he was thirty-one.

118
00:07:44,124 --> 00:07:47,514
He had worked odd jobs as
a gardener and a labourer.

119
00:07:47,544 --> 00:07:50,614
Homeless, with no money
and no family to turn to,

120
00:07:50,644 --> 00:07:51,984
he drifted to London.

121
00:07:52,834 --> 00:07:59,684
In January 1943, he was found in an abandoned
warehouse near King's Cross, seriously ill.

122
00:07:59,714 --> 00:08:02,564
He had eaten rat poison
containing phosphorus,

123
00:08:02,594 --> 00:08:05,144
a paste smeared on crusts of bread.

124
00:08:05,174 --> 00:08:09,504
Whether he meant to end his life, or
was simply starving, no one can say.

125
00:08:09,534 --> 00:08:11,324
He died in St Pancras Hospital.

126
00:08:11,354 --> 00:08:13,778
He was in his mid-thirties.

127
00:08:14,928 --> 00:08:17,378
No relatives were found to claim him.

128
00:08:17,408 --> 00:08:21,518
Montagu would later say that
the family had given permission.

129
00:08:21,548 --> 00:08:24,584
Historians have found no
evidence that they did.

130
00:08:25,734 --> 00:08:28,884
The man they invented to take
his place was very different.

131
00:08:28,914 --> 00:08:32,564
Captain, Acting Major,
William Martin, Royal Marines,

132
00:08:32,594 --> 00:08:35,464
attached to Combined
Operations Headquarters.

133
00:08:35,494 --> 00:08:40,284
Martin was a common enough name that several
real officers of about that rank shared it.

134
00:08:40,314 --> 00:08:43,064
As a Royal Marine, he
came under the Admiralty,

135
00:08:43,094 --> 00:08:47,224
so any inquiry about his death would
come back to Naval Intelligence.

136
00:08:47,254 --> 00:08:50,424
And an acting major was senior
enough to carry secrets,

137
00:08:50,454 --> 00:08:53,559
but junior enough that no
one would expect to know him.

138
00:08:54,409 --> 00:08:56,334
Then they gave him a life.

139
00:08:57,484 --> 00:09:00,954
In his pockets they placed
what spies call pocket litter:

140
00:09:00,984 --> 00:09:04,354
the small, ordinary
debris of a real person.

141
00:09:04,384 --> 00:09:07,214
A photograph of a fiancée named Pam.

142
00:09:07,244 --> 00:09:09,074
Two love letters from her.

143
00:09:09,104 --> 00:09:12,794
A receipt from a Bond Street jeweller
for a diamond engagement ring,

144
00:09:12,824 --> 00:09:17,047
priced at fifty-three pounds,
ten shillings and sixpence.

145
00:09:17,897 --> 00:09:19,807
A stern letter from his father.

146
00:09:19,837 --> 00:09:22,007
A note from the family solicitor.

147
00:09:22,037 --> 00:09:25,927
A demand from Lloyds Bank about an
overdraft of seventy-nine pounds,

148
00:09:25,957 --> 00:09:27,307
nineteen shillings and twopence.

149
00:09:27,337 --> 00:09:30,067
Theatre ticket stubs.

150
00:09:30,097 --> 00:09:33,167
A bill for four nights at
the Naval and Military Club.

151
00:09:33,197 --> 00:09:38,047
Keys, cigarettes, stamps, a silver
cross and a St Christopher medallion.

152
00:09:38,077 --> 00:09:40,527
Together, they traced a
man's last week in London,

153
00:09:40,557 --> 00:09:43,263
from the 18th to the 24th of April.

154
00:09:44,113 --> 00:09:46,083
Pam was not real either.

155
00:09:46,113 --> 00:09:50,070
The photograph was of an
MI5 clerk named Jean Leslie.

156
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:53,330
The identity card was harder.

157
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:56,910
Photographs of the dead man looked
exactly like what they were.

158
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:59,630
So the team searched for a
living man who resembled him,

159
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:03,250
and found one inside
MI5: Captain Ronnie Reed,

160
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:07,790
who agreed to be photographed
in a Royal Marine uniform.

161
00:10:07,820 --> 00:10:11,010
Montagu then spent weeks rubbing
the cards against his trousers,

162
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:12,826
so they would look worn.

163
00:10:13,676 --> 00:10:16,086
Even the uniform was broken in.

164
00:10:16,116 --> 00:10:20,806
Cholmondeley, who was about the same
build, wore it until it looked lived in.

165
00:10:20,836 --> 00:10:25,326
Piece by piece, a man who had died
almost unnoticed was being turned into

166
00:10:25,356 --> 00:10:28,702
an officer whose death the
enemy was meant to notice.

167
00:10:33,652 --> 00:10:36,182
Now came the documents that mattered.

168
00:10:36,212 --> 00:10:40,082
The key letter was written by
Lieutenant General Sir Archibald Nye,

169
00:10:40,112 --> 00:10:45,062
Vice Chief of the Imperial General
Staff, to General Sir Harold Alexander,

170
00:10:45,092 --> 00:10:48,062
who commanded the Allied
armies in Tunisia.

171
00:10:48,092 --> 00:10:51,482
After several drafts
failed to sound natural,

172
00:10:51,512 --> 00:10:53,792
Nye was asked to write it himself.

173
00:10:54,642 --> 00:10:58,152
It read like a private letter
between two senior officers:

174
00:10:58,182 --> 00:11:02,312
gossip, grievances, and
almost in passing, the plan.

175
00:11:02,342 --> 00:11:05,752
Allied forces, it suggested,
would land in Greece,

176
00:11:05,782 --> 00:11:08,392
near Cape Araxos and Kalamata.

177
00:11:08,422 --> 00:11:11,426
Sicily was mentioned, but
only as a cover target.

178
00:11:12,276 --> 00:11:15,926
A second letter, from Lord Louis
Mountbatten to Admiral Andrew Cunningham,

179
00:11:15,956 --> 00:11:20,526
introduced Major Martin as an
expert in amphibious warfare.

180
00:11:20,556 --> 00:11:23,926
It even included a clumsy
joke about sardines,

181
00:11:23,956 --> 00:11:28,126
which Montagu hoped the Germans
would read as a hint at Sardinia.

182
00:11:28,156 --> 00:11:31,546
And inside one letter, the
team placed a single eyelash.

183
00:11:31,576 --> 00:11:35,956
If it was missing when the letter came
back, they would know it had been opened.

184
00:11:36,806 --> 00:11:40,516
So the papers could not be
overlooked, they went into a briefcase,

185
00:11:40,546 --> 00:11:43,636
attached to Martin by a
leather-covered chain,

186
00:11:43,666 --> 00:11:47,683
the kind used by couriers, looped
around the belt of his trench coat.

187
00:11:48,533 --> 00:11:49,743
Then the place.

188
00:11:49,773 --> 00:11:53,543
Advised by the Admiralty's
hydrographer on tides and currents,

189
00:11:53,573 --> 00:11:57,983
they chose the port of Huelva, on
Spain's southern Atlantic coast.

190
00:11:58,013 --> 00:12:00,283
Spain was officially neutral.

191
00:12:00,313 --> 00:12:04,443
But Montagu knew that in Huelva
there was a very active German agent,

192
00:12:04,473 --> 00:12:08,563
Adolf Clauss of the Abwehr,
German military intelligence,

193
00:12:08,593 --> 00:12:11,724
with excellent contacts
among Spanish officials.

194
00:12:12,574 --> 00:12:16,324
On the 13th of April, the Chiefs
of Staff approved the plan.

195
00:12:16,354 --> 00:12:20,204
Two days later, Colonel John Bevan of
the London Controlling Section briefed

196
00:12:20,234 --> 00:12:21,824
Winston Churchill.

197
00:12:21,854 --> 00:12:25,304
According to Bevan, he found
the Prime Minister in bed,

198
00:12:25,334 --> 00:12:27,104
smoking a cigar.

199
00:12:27,134 --> 00:12:30,684
When Bevan warned that the Spanish
might simply hand the body back with the

200
00:12:30,714 --> 00:12:34,224
papers unread, Churchill
replied that, in that case,

201
00:12:34,254 --> 00:12:37,291
they would have to get the body
back and give it another swim.

202
00:12:38,141 --> 00:12:43,884
Final approval came from General
Eisenhower's headquarters on the 17th of April.

203
00:12:44,834 --> 00:12:48,224
In the early hours of that
same day, in a London mortuary,

204
00:12:48,254 --> 00:12:51,744
Glyndwr Michael was dressed
as Major William Martin.

205
00:12:51,774 --> 00:12:54,144
The pocket litter went into his pockets.

206
00:12:54,174 --> 00:12:56,164
The briefcase was attached.

207
00:12:56,194 --> 00:12:59,264
He was sealed in a steel
canister packed with dry ice,

208
00:12:59,294 --> 00:13:03,028
labelled: handle with
care, optical instruments.

209
00:13:03,878 --> 00:13:07,028
That night, a van driven
by a former racing driver,

210
00:13:07,058 --> 00:13:11,648
St John Horsfall, carried the canister
more than four hundred miles north

211
00:13:11,678 --> 00:13:15,268
through the blackout to
Greenock, on the River Clyde,

212
00:13:15,298 --> 00:13:18,189
with Montagu and
Cholmondeley riding in the back.

213
00:13:19,039 --> 00:13:23,189
There, it was taken aboard
the submarine HMS Seraph.

214
00:13:23,219 --> 00:13:27,689
Her commander, Lieutenant Bill Jewell,
told his crew the canister held a secret

215
00:13:27,719 --> 00:13:29,589
weather-reporting device.

216
00:13:30,439 --> 00:13:35,469
Seraph sailed on the 19th of April,
and was bombed twice on the way south.

217
00:13:35,499 --> 00:13:38,647
On the 29th, she reached
the waters off Huelva.

218
00:13:39,497 --> 00:13:43,407
At four fifteen in the morning
of April 30th, she surfaced.

219
00:13:43,437 --> 00:13:47,247
Jewell sent the crew below,
leaving only his officers on deck.

220
00:13:47,277 --> 00:13:50,887
They opened the canister and
lowered the body into the sea.

221
00:13:50,917 --> 00:13:53,307
Jewell read the thirty-ninth Psalm.

222
00:13:53,337 --> 00:13:57,207
Then he ordered the engines full astern,
and the wash of the propellers pushed

223
00:13:57,237 --> 00:13:58,000
the body toward the shore.

224
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:03,447
He signalled the Admiralty:
Mincemeat completed.

225
00:14:08,597 --> 00:14:12,367
In Spain, the plan now depended
on a chain of strangers.

226
00:14:12,397 --> 00:14:16,347
On May 1st, two Spanish doctors
carried out a post-mortem in Huelva,

227
00:14:16,377 --> 00:14:19,107
with the British vice-consul present.

228
00:14:19,137 --> 00:14:23,347
As the heat of the day rose, Haselden
suggested that the doctors might like

229
00:14:23,377 --> 00:14:25,507
to finish up and go to lunch.

230
00:14:25,537 --> 00:14:31,062
They agreed, and recorded the cause of death
as asphyxiation through immersion in the sea.

231
00:14:31,912 --> 00:14:36,522
The next day, Major William Martin was
buried in Huelva's cemetery with full

232
00:14:36,552 --> 00:14:38,121
military honours.

233
00:14:39,271 --> 00:14:42,621
The briefcase, though,
stayed with the Spanish navy.

234
00:14:42,651 --> 00:14:47,141
Despite pressure from Clauss, it was
not handed straight to the Germans.

235
00:14:47,171 --> 00:14:50,021
It went to the naval
headquarters at San Fernando,

236
00:14:50,051 --> 00:14:52,434
near Cádiz, and on to Madrid.

237
00:14:53,284 --> 00:14:56,294
In Madrid, a senior Abwehr
officer, Karl-Erich Kühlenthal,

238
00:14:56,324 --> 00:15:03,094
asked Admiral Wilhelm Canaris, the head of
German military intelligence, to intervene.

239
00:15:03,124 --> 00:15:06,934
The Spanish found a way to read the
letters without breaking the seals.

240
00:15:06,964 --> 00:15:10,394
They wound the damp paper tightly around
a thin probe and drew it out through

241
00:15:10,424 --> 00:15:12,754
the gap beneath the envelope flap.

242
00:15:12,784 --> 00:15:17,488
The letters were dried, photographed,
soaked in salt water again, and put back.

243
00:15:18,338 --> 00:15:22,388
The information was passed to
the Germans on the 8th of May.

244
00:15:22,418 --> 00:15:27,942
Kühlenthal considered it so important that
he took the documents to Germany himself.

245
00:15:28,892 --> 00:15:32,422
On the 11th of May, the briefcase
was returned to the British.

246
00:15:32,452 --> 00:15:34,742
In London, the letters were examined.

247
00:15:34,772 --> 00:15:36,662
The eyelash was gone.

248
00:15:36,692 --> 00:15:41,462
The paper fibres showed extra folding,
and as the still-damp letters dried,

249
00:15:41,492 --> 00:15:45,322
they curled back into the rolled shape
in which they had been pulled out.

250
00:15:45,352 --> 00:15:46,817
Someone had read them.

251
00:15:47,967 --> 00:15:52,417
To keep the Germans from suspecting a
trap, the British sent another message

252
00:15:52,447 --> 00:15:57,417
they knew would be intercepted, claiming
the envelopes had not been opened.

253
00:15:58,267 --> 00:16:01,197
Then came the confirmation
that mattered most.

254
00:16:01,227 --> 00:16:05,277
At Bletchley Park, codebreakers decrypted
a German message warning that the

255
00:16:05,307 --> 00:16:10,517
Allied invasion would come in the Balkans,
with a feint toward the Dodecanese.

256
00:16:10,547 --> 00:16:14,257
A signal went to Churchill,
then in the United States.

257
00:16:14,287 --> 00:16:19,502
Mincemeat, it said, had been swallowed
rod, line and sinker by the right people.

258
00:16:20,352 --> 00:16:24,142
On May 14th, Admiral
Karl Dönitz met Hitler.

259
00:16:24,172 --> 00:16:28,582
According to Dönitz's own record,
Hitler did not agree with Mussolini that

260
00:16:28,612 --> 00:16:31,562
Sicily was the most likely target.

261
00:16:31,592 --> 00:16:35,702
He believed the captured Anglo-Saxon
order confirmed that the attacks would

262
00:16:35,732 --> 00:16:39,143
be aimed mainly at Sardinia
and the Peloponnese.

263
00:16:39,993 --> 00:16:44,963
Hitler ordered that Greece, Sardinia
and Corsica be defended at all costs.

264
00:16:44,993 --> 00:16:49,383
The experienced 1st Panzer Division
was sent from France to Greece.

265
00:16:49,413 --> 00:16:52,603
By the end of June, German
strength on Sardinia had doubled,

266
00:16:52,633 --> 00:16:55,083
to about ten thousand men.

267
00:16:55,113 --> 00:16:59,135
German torpedo boats were moved
from Sicily to the Greek islands.

268
00:17:03,885 --> 00:17:08,055
On the 9th of July 1943,
the Allies invaded Sicily,

269
00:17:08,085 --> 00:17:12,830
in what was then the largest
amphibious assault of the war.

270
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:17,930
Intercepted German signals showed that,
even four hours after the landings

271
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:23,230
began, twenty-one aircraft were
leaving Sicily to reinforce Sardinia.

272
00:17:23,260 --> 00:17:27,450
Weeks later, Hitler was still
expecting a blow in the Balkans.

273
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:32,896
In late July, he sent Field Marshal Erwin
Rommel to Greece to prepare its defence.

274
00:17:33,746 --> 00:17:36,336
The fighting on Sicily was hard.

275
00:17:36,366 --> 00:17:41,136
Around sixty-five thousand German
troops held off a far larger Allied army

276
00:17:41,166 --> 00:17:45,156
long enough for many of them to
escape to the Italian mainland.

277
00:17:45,186 --> 00:17:48,079
But the island fell in thirty-eight days.

278
00:17:48,929 --> 00:17:52,879
According to the historian Ben
Macintyre, the British had expected around

279
00:17:52,909 --> 00:17:58,599
ten thousand casualties in the first week;
the real figure was about a seventh of that.

280
00:17:58,629 --> 00:18:02,379
The navy had prepared to lose
as many as three hundred ships.

281
00:18:02,409 --> 00:18:04,039
It lost twelve.

282
00:18:04,069 --> 00:18:08,183
A campaign expected to take ninety
days was over in thirty-eight.

283
00:18:09,033 --> 00:18:13,543
In the middle of the campaign, Mussolini
was voted down by his own Fascist

284
00:18:13,573 --> 00:18:16,585
Grand Council and removed from power.

285
00:18:17,635 --> 00:18:20,805
So did Operation Mincemeat
win the battle for Sicily?

286
00:18:20,835 --> 00:18:22,845
Historians are careful here.

287
00:18:22,875 --> 00:18:26,405
Michael Howard called it perhaps the most
successful single deception operation

288
00:18:26,435 --> 00:18:28,225
of the entire war.

289
00:18:28,255 --> 00:18:32,265
But he also argued that Hitler's
long-standing obsession with the Balkans

290
00:18:32,295 --> 00:18:35,225
mattered more than any single deception.

291
00:18:35,255 --> 00:18:39,065
Others point to Hitler's
distrust of his Italian allies.

292
00:18:39,095 --> 00:18:43,658
Macintyre writes that its exact
impact is impossible to calculate.

293
00:18:44,508 --> 00:18:46,458
What can be said is this.

294
00:18:46,488 --> 00:18:50,038
At the moment it mattered, German
attention, and German troops,

295
00:18:50,068 --> 00:18:52,318
were pointed in the wrong direction.

296
00:18:52,348 --> 00:18:54,897
And a dead man had
helped to put them there.

297
00:19:00,047 --> 00:19:02,717
After the war, the story
leaked into fiction,

298
00:19:02,747 --> 00:19:07,857
first in a 1950 novel by the former
cabinet minister Duff Cooper.

299
00:19:07,887 --> 00:19:12,717
British security decided the best answer
was to tell a version of the truth.

300
00:19:12,747 --> 00:19:16,817
Montagu wrote The Man
Who Never Was in 1953.

301
00:19:16,847 --> 00:19:19,934
It sold millions of
copies and became a film.

302
00:19:20,784 --> 00:19:24,174
But he never revealed who
the dead man really was.

303
00:19:24,204 --> 00:19:26,574
He described him as a
bit of a ne'er-do-well,

304
00:19:26,604 --> 00:19:30,294
and said that the only
worthwhile thing he ever did,

305
00:19:30,324 --> 00:19:31,944
he did after his death.

306
00:19:33,294 --> 00:19:38,064
In Huelva, a headstone stood over the
grave of a man who had never existed.

307
00:19:38,094 --> 00:19:42,244
It named his invented parents,
and ended with a line of Latin:

308
00:19:42,274 --> 00:19:45,348
it is sweet and fitting
to die for one's country.

309
00:19:46,198 --> 00:19:51,388
Then, in 1996, an amateur historian
named Roger Morgan found the answer in

310
00:19:51,418 --> 00:19:54,028
Britain's Public Record Office.

311
00:19:54,058 --> 00:19:56,568
The body was Glyndwr Michael.

312
00:19:56,598 --> 00:20:01,349
In the late 1990s, a new line
was added to the grave in Huelva.

313
00:20:02,199 --> 00:20:05,711
Glyndwr Michael served
as Major William Martin.

314
00:20:07,261 --> 00:20:11,311
Some researchers have since argued
that a different body was used,

315
00:20:11,341 --> 00:20:16,511
a sailor lost with the
carrier HMS Dasher in 1943.

316
00:20:16,541 --> 00:20:19,671
The official records of the
operation, and most historians,

317
00:20:19,701 --> 00:20:21,907
name Glyndwr Michael.

318
00:20:22,757 --> 00:20:27,867
In Aberbargoed, the town he left behind,
his name now stands on the war memorial,

319
00:20:27,897 --> 00:20:32,116
beneath a Welsh inscription:
the man who never was.

320
00:20:33,266 --> 00:20:35,636
He never wore a uniform in life.

321
00:20:35,666 --> 00:20:37,396
He never chose to serve.

322
00:20:37,426 --> 00:20:40,496
He could not have known
what was done in his name.

323
00:20:40,526 --> 00:20:45,376
But in the spring of 1943, a forgotten
man from the Welsh valleys carried

324
00:20:45,406 --> 00:20:49,807
one of the most consequential
deceptions of the war across the sea.

325
00:20:50,957 --> 00:20:53,967
The Germans believed in
Major William Martin.

326
00:20:53,997 --> 00:20:57,267
History, at last,
remembers Glyndwr Michael.
